1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,508
Numbers grow, one system inside another. Nested Number Systems.

2
00:00:05,508 --> 00:00:11,270
From Natural Numbers to Rational Numbers. Natural, Whole, Integer,

3
00:00:11,270 --> 00:00:15,931
Rational. How does one number system grow into another?

4
00:00:16,283 --> 00:00:19,301
Let's start with the simplest numbers — the natural

5
00:00:19,301 --> 00:00:23,021
numbers. One, two, three, four, five, and on it

6
00:00:23,021 --> 00:00:26,319
goes. These make up the set of Natural Numbers.

7
00:00:26,319 --> 00:00:28,214
But where does zero belong?

8
00:00:28,567 --> 00:00:31,951
All natural numbers are whole numbers — but whole

9
00:00:31,951 --> 00:00:35,995
numbers add one more. Watch zero join the family.

10
00:00:35,995 --> 00:00:39,792
Now we have zero, one, two, three, and on.

11
00:00:39,792 --> 00:00:44,744
Natural numbers are completely inside whole numbers. But numbers

12
00:00:44,744 --> 00:00:47,138
below zero are still missing.

13
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,574
Whole numbers grow again — this time into negative

14
00:00:50,574 --> 00:00:55,111
territory. Watch the negative numbers join: minus three, minus

15
00:00:55,111 --> 00:00:59,355
two, minus one. Integers add the negative numbers. Whole

16
00:00:59,355 --> 00:01:03,673
numbers are completely inside integers. But fractions are still

17
00:01:03,673 --> 00:01:04,551
missing.

18
00:01:04,917 --> 00:01:09,559
Now integers expand into rational numbers. Watch: five becomes

19
00:01:09,559 --> 00:01:13,041
five over one, minus three becomes minus three over

20
00:01:13,041 --> 00:01:16,987
one, and zero becomes zero over one. Rational numbers

21
00:01:16,987 --> 00:01:20,237
also fill in fractions between the integers — like

22
00:01:20,237 --> 00:01:24,725
one half, and minus three quarters. Rational Numbers include

23
00:01:24,725 --> 00:01:29,599
integers AND fractions. Integers are completely inside the rational

24
00:01:29,599 --> 00:01:30,528
numbers.

25
00:01:31,533 --> 00:01:35,145
Let's see the whole picture together. Natural numbers form

26
00:01:35,145 --> 00:01:39,226
the smallest circle. Whole numbers wrap around them. Integers

27
00:01:39,226 --> 00:01:42,972
wrap around whole numbers. And rational numbers contain them

28
00:01:42,972 --> 00:01:46,784
all. Every smaller system fits perfectly inside the next.

29
00:01:47,150 --> 00:01:50,232
Now it's your turn — where does each number

30
00:01:50,232 --> 00:01:56,221
belong? Number one: three. Number two: zero. Number three:

31
00:01:56,221 --> 00:02:00,008
minus one half. Pause and figure out which sets

32
00:02:00,008 --> 00:02:01,681
contain each one!

33
00:02:12,483 --> 00:02:17,077
Three is natural, so it's whole, integer, and rational

34
00:02:17,077 --> 00:02:20,788
too. Zero is not natural, but it is whole,

35
00:02:20,788 --> 00:02:25,382
integer, and rational. Minus one half is only rational

36
00:02:25,382 --> 00:02:27,414
— not whole or integer.

37
00:02:28,417 --> 00:02:31,664
Let's lock in the big picture. Watch the whole

38
00:02:31,664 --> 00:02:34,989
chain build. Natural is a subset of Whole, is

39
00:02:34,989 --> 00:02:38,314
a subset of Integer, is a subset of Rational.

40
00:02:38,314 --> 00:02:41,097
Thank you so much for watching, and I will

41
00:02:41,097 --> 00:02:43,108
see you in the next lesson!
